## Solution

We seek the Laurent series of $f(z) = \log\frac{z-a}{z-b}$ about $z_0 = \infty$.

**Setup.** Expanding about $\infty$ means working in powers of $1/z$. Factor $z$ from each term in the ratio:

$$
\frac{z-a}{z-b} = \frac{z(1-a/z)}{z(1-b/z)} = \frac{1-a/z}{1-b/z}.
$$

Hence

$$
f(z) = \log\!\left(1 - \frac{a}{z}\right) - \log\!\left(1 - \frac{b}{z}\right).
$$

**Convergence region.** The standard expansion $\log(1-u) = -\sum_{n=1}^{\infty} \frac{u^n}{n}$ is valid for $|u|<1$. Applying it to each logarithm requires

$$
\left|\frac{a}{z}\right| < 1 \quad\text{and}\quad \left|\frac{b}{z}\right| < 1,
$$

i.e. $|z| > \max(|a|,|b|)$. This is the annulus of convergence: $\{z : |z| > \max(|a|,|b|)\}$, an exterior neighborhood of $\infty$.

**Expansion.** For $|z| > \max(|a|,|b|)$:

$$
\log\!\left(1-\frac{a}{z}\right) = -\sum_{n=1}^{\infty}\frac{1}{n}\left(\frac{a}{z}\right)^n = -\sum_{n=1}^{\infty}\frac{a^n}{n\,z^n},
$$

$$
\log\!\left(1-\frac{b}{z}\right) = -\sum_{n=1}^{\infty}\frac{1}{n}\left(\frac{b}{z}\right)^n = -\sum_{n=1}^{\infty}\frac{b^n}{n\,z^n}.
$$

Subtracting:

$$
f(z) = \sum_{n=1}^{\infty}\frac{b^n - a^n}{n\,z^n}.
$$

**Consistency check.** As $z\to\infty$, $\frac{z-a}{z-b}\to 1$, so $f(z)\to \log 1 = 0$. The series has no constant term and no positive powers of $z$, confirming $f(\infty)=0$ and that $\infty$ is a removable-type point (the function is analytic at $\infty$ with value $0$). The series contains only negative powers of $z$ (i.e. positive powers of $1/z$), which is the expected form for a Laurent series about $\infty$.

**Result.** The Laurent series of $f(z)=\log\frac{z-a}{z-b}$ about $z_0=\infty$, valid for $|z|>\max(|a|,|b|)$, is

$$
\boxed{\,f(z) = \sum_{n=1}^{\infty}\frac{b^n - a^n}{n\,z^n}\,}
$$

or equivalently, writing $w = 1/z$,

$$
f(z) = \sum_{n=1}^{\infty}\frac{b^n-a^n}{n}\,w^n, \qquad |w| < \frac{1}{\max(|a|,|b|)}.
$$

